<!-- cell: title-page id="aa409a20-450d-437c-86f7-61346f59e8bf" title="The Night Train" -->

<!-- cell: markdown id="ef29ef6f-854e-4511-b0c4-15563d39e5d3" -->

The night train from Prague to Vienna left at 22:14, and Dana Kessler was in seat 31 of car 7 by 22:10, with the drive taped beneath the insole of her left boot.

She had carried things for Anton for six years. Documents, money once, a small sealed box she had never asked about. Anton had found her when she had nothing, and she had never given him a reason to doubt her. That, she had always assumed, was the arrangement.

At 23:02 her phone buzzed. Car 9, compartment 4. Hand it to the man with the newspaper. Then go home. A.

She walked the corridor slowly, the train swaying under her. The carriages smelled of coffee and wet coats. In car 9, through the glass of compartment 4, she saw him: grey coat, folded newspaper, an expensive steel watch with a red second hand.

She stopped.

She had seen that watch before, in Kraków, on the wrist of one of the two men who had walked out of a café a minute before her contact was found dead in it. She had told Anton about the watch. She had told only Anton.

The train rocked through the dark. Dana stood very still and let the knowledge settle: there was no mistake, and no other explanation. Anton had sold the drive, and he had sold her with it. The man with the newspaper was not there to collect. He was there to finish.

She went back to the toilet in car 8 and locked the door. With hands that would not quite stop shaking, she peeled the drive out of her boot and pushed it deep into the lining of her coat. From her wallet she took the dead hotel key card from Kraków, wrapped it in the same black tape, and slid it into a matchbox.

Then she knocked on compartment 4.

"From Anton," she said, and held out the matchbox. The man smiled and did not look at it. His eyes were on her hands.

"Sit," he said.

"Two minutes," said Dana. "Toilet."

The train was slowing for Břeclav. She walked, not ran, to the end of car 9, opened the door onto the cold, and stepped down onto the platform as the brakes hissed. She did not look back until the train had gone.

In the morning she posted the drive to an address in Zurich that Anton did not know existed, and then she bought a new phone and threw the old one in the river.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="a4381e2e-8e5f-4c53-ae89-46b460d44962" title="Dana Kessler" words="0-32, 33-41, 377-386" -->

A courier who has carried things for Anton for six years without asking questions, and who discovers on the night train that she was meant to die on it.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="e877d0dd-64c8-4285-b9aa-892ce60ff9c4" title="Anton" words="54-72, 81-101, 216-227" -->

Dana's handler, who took her in when she had nothing, and who has sold both the drive and her.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="2f586fee-4adb-47d1-ae05-f36be8db2433" title="The night train from Prague to Vienna" words="102-119, 193-198, 345-350" -->

The train on which the handover and the escape play out, from car 7 to the platform at Břeclav.

<!-- cell: salient-concept id="4ee5b1ad-9df8-4272-ada4-0e9dc893a0e6" title="Betrayal" words="181-192, 228-242, 387-419" -->

Anton's betrayal, which Dana reads in a stranger's watch: only Anton knew she had seen it.

<!-- cell: plot id="11d040d0-2ce8-4ad8-b29b-711e404d0cce" title="The handover" words="0-32, 81-101, 120-144, 147-192, 216-227, 256-306, 313-321, 345-376, 387-419" -->

Told to hand the drive to a man on the train, Dana recognises his watch from a killing in Kraków, realises Anton has betrayed her, hands over a decoy and leaves the train at Břeclav with the real drive.
